ChemDraw by Julian Toler
=====================
Freeware! feel free to hack the source code and if you make
improvements (I mean keep it small but make it better!) send
a copy back to Steve Lichfield at 3Lib.

The program is a type 0 OPA (i.e. it does not understand file
commands sent to it from the system screen).  I consider that
this is in keeping with the way the program is designed to
work and besides it helps to keep down the file size!

The help file (CHEMHELP.OPO) should be in the \CHEM
directory.  This file can be deleted without problems if
required to save space. 

Quick Overview
==============
The program treats the screen as workspace, upon which
the chemically minded user can create molecules of his or
her choice.  I made this program as a notepad to remember
the structure of a few important (to my business) molecules.
When saving a newly created molecule, the program will
offer the user the opportunity to add the picture to a catalogue
file (\CHEM\CHEMBASE.ODB).  This catalogue function allows
the user to flip through a number of pictures rather like a standard
Psion database file.  It even has similar control keys (e.g. Enter
to scroll through the file, Shift-Enter to scroll backwards, and search
functions to track down  relevant associated text).

The catalogue function acts like a program-within-a-program and
the user can add new pictures from scratch when working in this
mode (it will of course handle any pictures, not just chemical
structures, but due to the limited features, in this program, only
416 x 140 pixels can be viewed).  The notes/description info
in catalogue mode can be switched of if not required.

A wide varity of pre-formed chemically orientated picture clips
have been made available (in the \CHEM\OBJECTS directory).
These are accessed from the program using the Bond, Moeity
and Large Symbol functions (see the Menu).  Objects can be
positioned anywhere on the screen.  Movement on screen
is effected using the cursor (arrow) and Psion-cursor keys.

INSTALLATION
============
Create a directory called "CHEM" and copy all example PIC files
into this directory, as well as the file CHEMBASE.ODB (this is not
critical as the file will be created here if not found.

As a sub directory of CHEM, create a subdirectory called "OBJECTS"
(e.g. M:\CHEM\OBJECTS) and copy all object files into it
i.e. OBJ1.PIC to OBJ87.PIC.  These are the molecule, bond, and
moeity files to which the program refers when creating new
structures.  These may be called up by any painting program
and edited if desired, but remember the references in the
program will have to be adjusted accordingly.

Copy CHEMDRAW.OPA to any APP directory and install from the
system screen as usual.
HELP
====
Here is the help file with programming code chopped out:

Help: ChemDraw
˛General principles
˛Menu options
˛Catalogue feature
˛Hints & techniques
˛Special characters

Help: General Principles
ChemDraw is a chemically orientated drawing package.
The main feature is a set of pre-drawn templates for
adding rings and bonds.  The ability to cut, save and
paste enables the user to build up a library of customised
building blocks for future use and the catalogue feature
allows these to be quickly searched, viewed and pasted.

Help: Menu
˛File
˛Draw
˛Erase
˛Information

Help: Catalogue
The catalogue functions acts like a database for
your picture files. In fact it will load, catalogue and
show any picture file you may wish to keep track of
on any disk or directory.  If you delete a PIC file
outside of the program, an info message will tell you
that the picture file cannot be located.
more information on the catalogue function
Use Enter and Shift-Enter to scan forwards and back through
the catalogue or use Psion-q to search for a name etc.
The catalogue file is called ""chembase.odb"" and may be viewed
and edited like any other Psion Data file. It is located in
the \CHEM\ directory. If viewed using the Psion Data application
you will not see the pictures, just file names and descriptions.

Help: File Menu
˛Save
˛Retrieve
˛Exit

Help: File Save
Provides a rubber band box to select area to save to file.
A (very) small cursor will be seen. This may be obscured
by your drawing so move it around a bit until it is visible.
Press enter to set the top left corner of the save box,
then drag out a box enclosing the area to be saved. Press
enter again to complete the area selection.

Help: File Retrieve
Retrieve a PIC file and paste anywhere on the screen.
The picture does not have to be a chemical structure
but it would seem logical to restrict the size of
picture to an area similar to the work area of ChemDraw.

Help: File Exit
In Draw mode, Psion-x exits from the program.
In Catalogue mode, Psion-x returns you to Draw.
(to exit the program directly from catalogue mode
hit Psion-shift-X. Beware, no file will be saved!).

Help: Draw Menu

Polygons:
Benzene rings, cyclo-hexane, pentane, butane, & propane.

Bonds:
Pre-formed bonds are available set at various angles.

Moieties:
Common molecular sub-units, especially those needing subscripts.
more info on symbols, lines, text and brush

Large symbols:
A variety of large symbols such as brackets and arrows
are available.

Line:
Single or continuous lines may be drawn. Fix a point on a continuous
line using the space bar.  Terminate a line by pressing Enter.

Text:
Large, medium and small fonts are available in various styles.
Use small for subscripts etc. See Special character menu for
codes for negative (Ó), degree (¯) and other symbols.
Brush:
Hold Shift and move cursor to paint. Use +/- to increase/decrease
Hit Escape to return to normal mode.

Help: Erase Menu
Clear screen
Beware, doesn't save anything!
Eraser
Provides a variable size square eraser for tidying up your
pictures. Increase/decrease the size of the eraser by using
+/- on the keyboard. Activate the eraser by holding down Shift
and moving the cursor. Quit erase mode by pressing Escape.

Help: Information Menu
Show coordinates:
Provides x,y position information when pasting etc.

Help:
This help system. If the file CHEMHELP.OPO is not found
a polite message is shown (delete file if memory is short!).

About:
A brief advert for yours truly.

Help: Hints & Techniques
For accelerated cursor movement - hold down Psion key.
Pasting objects - after selecting an object to paste
it will appear on screen with the message ""Hit Enter
to Paste"". You are free to move the object on the screen
using the cursor or Psion-cursor keys before pasting.

Heterocycles: add the hetero atom as text in opaque mode.
This will ensue it hides the underlying ring.
Lines: to draw a series of connected lines, after drawing
each line, start the next without moving the cursor.

Help: Special characters
These can be entered using the text option
and may be mixed with normal text. E.g.
for a Phenyl symbol ù, hold Control while
typing 155. For OHÓ, type OH as normal then
hold Control and type 238 for the minus sign.
